## The 6 Checks (run in order; STOP on first FAIL unless `--force`)

### Check 1 — Drift verdict (REVIEW present)

**Drift verdict existence** (ADR-006): Read `{{ config.work_docs.dir }}REVIEW-{slug}.md` frontmatter.
- `drift_verdict` present AND `task_slug` matches current PLAN → **PASS**.
- `drift_verdict` absent OR `task_slug` mismatch → **FAIL**: `{{ stage_invocation("BLOCKED: check 1 (drift) — run /hm:review first", is_codex | default(false)) }}`.

This check is mechanical on purpose. The LLM PLAN/SPEC coverage judgement that used to sit here
as "1b" is gone (PLAN-workflow-steps-vs-model-capability ADR-003). Per-scenario coverage is
enforced where the tests are written: `/hm:execute` Phase A authors one test per SPEC scenario
and Phase A.5's coverage lens blocks implementation on any `scenarios_missing` (A.5 is a
TUNE-class gate graded `**` — Side-preset evidence only; see the
step-sensitivity registry); `/hm:wrapup` Step 3.5 writes the covering test ids back into the machine SPEC
(`pending_test`). Test-expressed PLAN exit criteria are re-run by Check 2 below; a script or
manual-checklist exit criterion has **no** automated re-check at this stage, and Check 6 gates the SPEC operation, not scenario coverage.
**`--no-tdd` / `lane: light` path:** `--no-tdd` skips Phase A/A.5 and `lane: light` skips A.5; this stage does not
re-derive scenario coverage.{% if strictness_of(config) == 'block' %}{% if config.preset == 'Production' %} The mechanical backstop is `/hm:wrapup` Step 3.5's
`spec_machine find-unbound` gate (fail-closed, STOP on this preset): a closed-type AC still `pending_test`
after the write-back cannot be committed, whichever way execute ran.{% else %} `/hm:wrapup` Step 3.5's
`spec_machine find-unbound` scan runs on this preset too but is **advisory only** (Side never STOPs),
so an AC still `pending_test` after `--no-tdd` is surfaced in the wrapup report, not blocked.{% endif %}{% else %} Task-driven mode has no machine
SPEC to scan, so on that path an uncovered scenario reaches wrapup unflagged — the accepted
cost of `--no-tdd` without a SPEC contract.{% endif %}

### Check 2 — Regression smoke

**One verb runs the gates and owns the marker.** It takes the gates from the project's CI (a
narrower guess passes locally, fails on push) and marks only a pass on an unchanged tree.

{% if is_codex %}
```
Bash("uv run --with {{ harness_maker_src_path }} hm observability.verification_cache run --root . --mode relevant")
```
{% else %}
```bash
!uv run --with {{ harness_maker_src_path }} hm observability.verification_cache run --root . --mode relevant --timeout-s 3600 --deadline-s 3600
```
{% endif %}

{% if is_codex %}Foreground call: the defaults (540 s/gate, 570 s total) report their own timeout.{% else %}Run it in the **background** (`run_in_background: true`): a full suite outlives the 10-minute
foreground cap.{% endif %} Branch on the `"exit"` field of the JSON line it prints, never on the
host's status; no JSON line = not a pass.

- `0` → PASS (`cached: true` = its own marker reused). Skip to Check 3.
- `1` → a gate failed or timed out, not a pass — stderr names the command and its output tail.
- `4` → the tree changed during the run, not a pass (stderr lists the paths); re-run.
- Killed by the host (no exit code) → not a pass, no marker.
- `3` → degraded: no readable CI (stderr says why). Only then:

<!-- @hm:verify-degraded -->
fall back to the project's toolchain (`pytest -q` / `ruff check .` / `ruff format --check .` / `mypy --strict`; `cargo test`; `pnpm test`) and say the gates were guessed. If the harness has its own `.claude-verify.sh phase_<N>` script, prefer it. After every command passes — and only then — write the marker:

{% if is_codex %}
```
Bash("uv run --with {{ harness_maker_src_path }} hm observability.verification_cache mark-pass --root . --mode relevant --checks lint,format,mypy,pytest")
```
{% else %}
```bash
!uv run --with {{ harness_maker_src_path }} hm observability.verification_cache mark-pass --root . --mode relevant --checks lint,format,mypy,pytest
```
{% endif %}
